---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MethodProvenance from "../../components/MethodProvenance.astro";
const defaultCode = `f <- function(x) {
  (x[1]^2 + x[2] - 11)^2 +
  (x[1] + x[2]^2 - 7)^2
}

x0 <- c(0, 0)`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Simulated Annealing — CMNA" description="Watch simulated annealing explore an arbitrary two-dimensional R objective.">
<header class="page-head"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Optimization · CMNA Laboratory</p><h1 class="page-title">Sometimes the algorithm should make a bad move on purpose.</h1><p class="lede">Define a two-dimensional objective in R, then watch simulated annealing accept, reject, wander, cool, and remember the best point it has seen.</p></div></header>
<section class="section"><div class="shell two-column"><article class="content"><h2>Escaping local minima costs something.</h2><p>At high temperature, uphill moves can survive. As the temperature falls, the algorithm becomes increasingly conservative. The randomness is not noise added after the fact; it is part of the search strategy.</p></article><aside class="callout"><strong>Himmelblau is perfect for this.</strong><p>Its multiple minima make it obvious why a method that can occasionally move uphill behaves differently from pure local descent.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="section lab-section"><div class="shell" data-sa-lab>
<div class="lab-heading"><div><p class="section-kicker">Bring your own landscape</p><h2 class="section-title">Let R wander before it settles.</h2><p class="lede">Write f(x) and x0 in R, choose temperature, cooling rate, and seed, then play the accepted and rejected moves.</p></div><div class="lab-expression"><span>Runtime</span><code data-status>Preparing R…</code></div></div>
<div class="r-guided-grid"><section class="r-guided-editor"><div class="r-editor-label"><span>R</span><small>Define <code>f</code> and two-dimensional <code>x0</code></small></div><textarea class="r-editor" data-r-code spellcheck="false">{defaultCode}</textarea><div class="r-guided-controls"><label>temperature<input type="number" value="20" min="1" step="any" data-temp /></label><label>cooling rate<input type="number" value="0.03" min="0" max="1" step="any" data-rate /></label><label>seed<input type="number" value="42" step="1" data-seed /></label></div><div class="r-run-row"><button class="button r-run" type="button" data-run disabled>Anneal in R</button><button class="r-reset" type="button" data-reset>Reset R session</button></div></section><aside class="r-guided-primer"><h3>Change only the seed.</h3><p>The same objective and cooling schedule can discover different basins because the search is stochastic. Then change the cooling rate and see how quickly exploration gives way to exploitation.</p></aside></div>
<div class="lab-summary" data-summary></div><div class="lab-plot-frame"><svg class="lab-plot gradient-plot" role="img" aria-label="Simulated annealing path across objective surface" data-plot></svg></div>
<div class="lab-stepper"><button class="button secondary-on-light" type="button" data-play disabled>Play annealing</button><label><span data-step-label>Iteration</span><input type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" data-step disabled /></label></div><p class="lab-status" data-step-detail></p>
<div class="lab-table-wrap"><table class="lab-table"><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>temperature</th><th>proposal f</th><th>accepted?</th><th>best f</th></tr></thead><tbody data-rows></tbody></table></div>
</div></section><script is:inline type="module" src="/js/simulated-annealing-lab.js"></script>
  <MethodProvenance
    book="§6.3.3 · Simulated Annealing · p. 187"
    functions={["sa"]}
    sources={["R/sa.R"]}
    teachingHref="/teaching/chapters/#chapter-6" zoo={[{ label: "Himmelblau's function", href: "/zoo/himmelblau-function/" }]}
  />

</BaseLayout>